<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Merged into the generated Info.plist by the Tauri bundler.

  NSMicrophoneUsageDescription is not optional and it is not boilerplate. Without it
  macOS does not show a permission dialog that the user then declines — the process is
  TERMINATED the moment it asks for the microphone. And this string IS the dialog: it
  is the only explanation a church volunteer ever gets for why the app wants to listen
  to their service.

  So it is written for them, not for us, and it answers the question they are actually
  asking — "is this thing recording our church?" It says what Relay does with the audio
  and, just as importantly, what it does not do with it. That claim is true and is the
  same one PRIVACY.md makes: transcription happens on this machine, and nothing leaves
  it (docs/PRIVACY.md; telemetry is opt-in, off by default, and never carries audio,
  transcript or verse text).

  If that ever stops being true, this string must change first.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Relay listens to the service so it can find the scripture being preached and put it on your screens. The audio is transcribed on this computer and is never sent anywhere.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
